@Tutorial(time: 60) {
    @Intro(title: "Complex Model Transformations") {
        Master complex transformation scenarios including refining mode, multiple source/target models, and sophisticated rule coordination.

        In this tutorial, you'll tackle real-world transformation challenges that require advanced ATL features and careful architectural design.

        You'll learn how to handle model refactoring, multi-model transformations, and complex metamodel mappings.

        @Image(source: "atl-transformations.svg", alt: "ATL Transformations")
    }

    @Section(title: "Refining Mode Transformations") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Refining mode enables in-place transformations where unmatched elements are automatically copied to the output. This is useful for model refactoring and partial transformations.

            Declare refining mode in the module header with `refining` in place of `from`, as in `create OUT: Model refining IN: Model;`. The target model OUT starts as a copy of the source model IN and conforms to the same metamodel.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create the metamodel that the refining transformations read and write.

                @Code(name: "Model.ecore", file: atl-07-step-01-model-metamodel.ecore)

                A refining transformation reads and writes models of one metamodel. A Container holds its Elements in `elements` and the Summaries that a lazy rule adds in `summaries`. An Element has a `name`, an integer `version` and a `deprecated` flag, and a Summary records the `elementName` of an element. Save it as Model.ecore, where the `-- @path` directives of the transformations find it.
            }

            @Step {
                Understand refining mode syntax.

                @Code(name: "RefiningExample.atl", file: atl-07-step-02-refining-mode.atl)

                The header `create OUT: Model refining IN: Model;` declares a refining transformation, which automatically copies unmatched elements. Without any rules, OUT is an unchanged copy of IN.
            }

            @Step {
                Refactor model elements selectively.

                @Code(name: "RefiningExample.atl", file: atl-07-step-03-selective-refactoring.atl)

                Only matched elements are transformed; others pass through unchanged. In refining mode the target pattern element `t` denotes the matched element itself, so the rule raises the version of each element older than version 2 in place.
            }

            @Step {
                Combine matched and lazy rules in refining mode.

                @Code(name: "RefiningExample.atl", file: atl-07-step-04-refining-with-lazy.atl)

                Use lazy rules to create new elements whilst refactoring existing ones. The matched rule SummariseContainer refines each container in place, calling the lazy rule CreateSummary for each of its elements to add a new Summary to its summaries.
            }

            @Step {
                Build a complete refactoring transformation.

                @Code(name: "RefiningExample.atl", file: atl-07-step-05-complete-refactoring.atl)

                This transformation demonstrates practical model refactoring using refining mode: it renames each deprecated element and clears its flag, and copies every other element unchanged.
            }

            @Step {
                Create a sample model with three elements to refine.

                @Code(name: "model.xmi", file: atl-07-step-06-sample-model.xmi)

                The Formatter is the only deprecated element. The Parser and the Scheduler set no `deprecated` flag, so it reads as false, the default of a Boolean attribute.
            }

            @Step {
                Validate the new files and refine the sample model with the complete refactoring.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: atl-07-step-07-run-refining.sh)

                `--source IN=model.xmi` binds the source model IN of the header to the sample model, and `--target OUT=refined.xmi` names the file that receives the refined copy OUT. swift-atl takes refining mode from the header and finds `Model.ecore` beside the transformation through its `-- @path` directive.
            }

            @Step {
                Look at the refined model that the transformation wrote.

                @Code(name: "refined.xmi", file: atl-07-step-08-refined.xmi)

                Only the Formatter has changed: it is now Updated_Formatter and no longer deprecated. The Parser and the Scheduler, which no rule matches, are copied as they are.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Multiple Source Models") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Complex transformations may consume multiple source models. The module header names each source model with its metamodel, as in `create OUT: Target from INA: ModelA, INB: ModelB;`.

            Rules refer to the types of a source model through its metamodel, such as `ModelA!ElementA`, and `allInstancesFrom` selects the instances of one model by its name.

            A rule may also take one element from each source model, as in `from a: ModelA!ElementA, b: ModelB!ElementB`. Such a source pattern matches every combination of its elements, so for 2 elements of the first kind and 3 of the second it matches 6 pairs. Usually only some pairs belong together, for example those in which one element refers to the other by a key, and a guard after the last pattern element, such as `(b.refId = a.id)`, keeps exactly those.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create the metamodel of the first source model.

                @Code(name: "ModelA.ecore", file: atl-07-step-09-modela-metamodel.ecore)

                An ElementA has a `name` and an integer `id`, by which the elements of the second source model refer to it. Save it as ModelA.ecore, where the `-- @path` directives of the transformations find it.
            }

            @Step {
                Create the metamodel of the second source model.

                @Code(name: "ModelB.ecore", file: atl-07-step-10-modelb-metamodel.ecore)

                An ElementB has a `name` and a `refId`, the id of the ElementA that it belongs to. Save it as ModelB.ecore beside ModelA.ecore.
            }

            @Step {
                Create the target metamodel that combines the two source models.

                @Code(name: "Target.ecore", file: atl-07-step-11-target-metamodel.ecore)

                The rules of this section create Items and Combined elements. An Item has a `name` and the `source` model that it comes from, and the coordinating rule fills `nameFromA` and `relatedB`, which receives one name for each related ElementB and so is a many-valued `EString` attribute. A Combined element holds `nameA`, `nameB` and the `combined` text. Save it as Target.ecore.
            }

            @Step {
                Set up multiple source models.

                @Code(name: "MultiSourceExample.atl", file: atl-07-step-12-multiple-sources.atl)

                Transformations can read from multiple source models simultaneously. The header binds INA to ModelA and INB to ModelB, and every type in a rule names its metamodel, never its model. Save the module as MultiSourceExample.atl, after its name.
            }

            @Step {
                Coordinate rules across models.

                @Code(name: "MultiSourceExample.atl", file: atl-07-step-13-cross-model-rules.atl)

                Complex transformations coordinate multiple rules that work across different models. For each ElementA, `ModelB!ElementB.allInstancesFrom('INB')` collects the elements of the source model INB, and the rule keeps the names of those whose refId is the id of the ElementA.
            }

            @Step {
                Build a complete multi-model transformation.

                @Code(name: "MultiSourceExample.atl", file: atl-07-step-14-complete-multi-model.atl)

                TransformA and TransformB turn the elements of each source model into Items. CombineModels takes one element from each model, and its guard `(b.refId = a.id)` keeps only the pairs in which the ElementB refers to the ElementA, so it creates one Combined element for each ElementB that belongs to an ElementA. Without the guard, it would create one for every pair, whether or not the two elements are related. CombineModels matches pairs, not single elements, so it does not conflict with TransformA or TransformB.
            }

            @Step {
                Create a sample model for the first source model.

                @Code(name: "modela.xmi", file: atl-07-step-15-sample-modela.xmi)

                The Kitchen and the Garden are two rooms, each with its id. A model with more than one root element wraps them in an `xmi:XMI` element.
            }

            @Step {
                Create a sample model for the second source model.

                @Code(name: "modelb.xmi", file: atl-07-step-16-sample-modelb.xmi)

                Three materials name the room they belong to by its id: the Tiles and the Paint belong to the Kitchen, and the Seeds to the Garden.
            }

            @Step {
                Validate the new files and run the complete transformation on both sample models.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: atl-07-step-17-run-multiple-sources.sh)

                The transformation reads two source models, so the command gives one `--source` option for each: `INA=modela.xmi` and `INB=modelb.xmi` bind the models of the header to the sample models, and `--target OUT=target.xmi` names the file that receives the model OUT.
            }

            @Step {
                Look at the target model that the transformation wrote.

                @Code(name: "target.xmi", file: atl-07-step-18-target.xmi)

                Each room and each material becomes an Item that records its source model. CombineModels created three Combined elements, one for each material with the room that it belongs to: the Tiles and the Paint with the Kitchen, and the Seeds with the Garden. Of the six pairs of a room and a material, the guard rejected the three in which the material belongs to the other room.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Multiple Target Models") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Complex transformations may also produce multiple target models. The module header names each target model with its metamodel, as in `create OUTA: TargetA, OUTB: TargetB from IN: Source;`.

            Each element that a rule creates goes to the target model of the metamodel of its type, so one rule can create elements in several target models.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create the source metamodel of the transformation.

                @Code(name: "Source.ecore", file: atl-07-step-19-source-metamodel.ecore)

                An Element has a `name`. Save it as Source.ecore, where the `-- @path` directives of the transformation find it.
            }

            @Step {
                Create the metamodel of the first target model.

                @Code(name: "TargetA.ecore", file: atl-07-step-20-targeta-metamodel.ecore)

                An ItemA has a `name`. Save it as TargetA.ecore beside Source.ecore.
            }

            @Step {
                Create the metamodel of the second target model.

                @Code(name: "TargetB.ecore", file: atl-07-step-21-targetb-metamodel.ecore)

                An ItemB has a `title` in place of a name. Save it as TargetB.ecore beside the other metamodels.
            }

            @Step {
                Generate multiple target models.

                @Code(name: "MultiTargetExample.atl", file: atl-07-step-22-multiple-targets.atl)

                Save this module as MultiTargetExample.atl, after its name, so that it does not replace the module of the previous section. ToTargets creates an ItemA, which goes to OUTA, and an ItemB, which goes to OUTB, from each Element. A single rule with two target pattern elements does this, because only one matched rule may transform a source element: two rules that both match `Source!Element` would stop the run with a rule conflict.
            }

            @Step {
                Create a sample model with two elements to transform.

                @Code(name: "source.xmi", file: atl-07-step-23-sample-source.xmi)

                The two shifts are Elements with a name each. A model with more than one root element wraps them in an `xmi:XMI` element.
            }

            @Step {
                Validate the new files and run the transformation on the sample model.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: atl-07-step-24-run-multiple-targets.sh)

                The transformation writes two target models, so the command gives one `--target` option for each: `OUTA=targeta.xmi` and `OUTB=targetb.xmi` name the files that receive the models of the header, and `--source IN=source.xmi` binds the source model to the sample.
            }

            @Step {
                Look at the first target model that the transformation wrote.

                @Code(name: "targeta.xmi", file: atl-07-step-25-targeta.xmi)

                OUTA holds one ItemA for each Element, with the name of the Element.
            }

            @Step {
                Look at the second target model that the transformation wrote.

                @Code(name: "targetb.xmi", file: atl-07-step-26-targetb.xmi)

                OUTB holds the corresponding ItemB for each Element, with the name of the Element as its title.
            }
        }
    }

    @Assessments {
        @MultipleChoice {
            What is refining mode used for?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                In-place transformations that copy unmatched elements

                @Justification(reaction: "Correct!") {
                    Refining mode automatically copies elements that don't match any rule, making it ideal for model refactoring where you only want to change specific elements.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Improving transformation performance

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite!") {
                    Whilst refining mode may affect performance, its primary purpose is enabling partial transformations, not optimisation.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Transforming between incompatible metamodels

                @Justification(reaction: "Incorrect!") {
                    Refining mode requires compatible source and target metamodels since unmatched elements are copied.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            Can you mix refining mode with lazy rules?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                Yes, lazy rules work normally in refining mode

                @Justification(reaction: "That's right!") {
                    Lazy rules function the same way in refining mode. The only difference is that unmatched elements are automatically copied.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                No, refining mode disables lazy rules

                @Justification(reaction: "Incorrect!") {
                    Lazy rules work perfectly in refining mode. They're invoked explicitly regardless of the transformation mode.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Only if you declare them specially

                @Justification(reaction: "Not required!") {
                    Lazy rules use the same syntax in both normal and refining modes.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            A rule has the source pattern `a: ModelA!ElementA, b: ModelB!ElementB` and no guard. The first source model holds 2 ElementA elements and the second 3 ElementB elements. How many times does the rule match?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                3 times, once for each ElementB

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite!") {
                    The rule does not pair each ElementB with one related ElementA by itself. Without a guard, nothing relates the two pattern elements.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                6 times, once for every pair of an ElementA and an ElementB

                @Justification(reaction: "Correct!") {
                    A source pattern with several elements matches every combination of them, 2 × 3 = 6 pairs here. A guard such as `(b.refId = a.id)` keeps only the pairs that belong together.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                5 times, once for each element of either model

                @Justification(reaction: "Incorrect!") {
                    That is how often two single-element rules such as TransformA and TransformB match together. A rule with two pattern elements matches pairs.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            How do you specify which target model to create elements in?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                By the order of rule definitions

                @Justification(reaction: "Try again!") {
                    Rule order doesn't determine target models. The type of each target pattern element does.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                By the metamodel of the type of the target pattern element

                @Justification(reaction: "Exactly!") {
                    Each target model conforms to its own metamodel, so an element of type `TargetA!ItemA` goes to OUTA and one of type `TargetB!ItemB` goes to OUTB, even when the same rule creates both.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                By prefixing the type with the target model name

                @Justification(reaction: "Incorrect!") {
                    The name before the `!` of a type always names a metamodel, such as TargetA, never a model such as OUTA.
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
